+# ── DNA: warning ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
+# STAGE 2. A vent is the only object in this world whose whole job is
+# CONSEQUENCE, and the interesting variable is not how fast it emits — a
+# still cannot see a rate — but how much the room tells you before the
+# emitting costs you anything. Three of these are three different claims
+# about whether the world is fair:
+#
+#   beacon  a four-metre pillar you can read from the doorway. The world
+#           warns you in advance and expects you to have looked.
+#   none    the orb alone, at ankle height, unlit from a distance. The
+#           rule is identical; nobody wrote it down.
+#   stain   a discolouration pressed into the floor and nothing above it.
+#           The warning exists but it is addressed to your feet: you can
+#           only read it from a place you are already standing.
+#   cage    bars and a hoop. Someone knew, filed it, fenced it — and the
+#           wave emits on exactly the same clock through the bars. A world
+#           that DOCUMENTS its hazards is not a world that contains them.
+#   shroud  an opaque cover over the seam. The world knows and has decided
+#           you should not. The quietest tile and the loudest claim.
+#
+# APPEARANCE ONLY, and deliberately so: emit_interval_s, wave_size,
+# start_delay_s, require_catalyst_armed, the spawn position and the lease
+# wind-down are byte-identical across all five. A vent that hides itself
+# is not a gentler vent. That is the point of the axis.
+#
+# `beacon` IS the legacy body: _build_beacon() adds pillar, halo, orb in
+# that order with every constant lifted unchanged from the original
+# _build_visual(), and registers the same three dim handles in the same
+# order, so the lease wind-down dims exactly what it dimmed before.
+const WARNING_VALUES: Array[String] = ["beacon", "none", "stain", "cage", "shroud"]
+@export_enum("beacon", "none", "stain", "cage", "shroud") var warning: String = "beacon"
+
